<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmarticle|my-notes>>

<\body>
  <section|Quick Start>

  <\summary>
    This file is a clean demo for <notes-code|my-notes>. If the package is
    installed correctly, you should see rendered callout blocks rather than a
    blank page or only source tags.
  </summary>

  <\warning>
    If the document appears blank, first restart TeXmacs and reopen this
    file. If you only see blue tags such as <notes-command|definition> or
    <notes-command|theorem>, turn off source editing with
    <notes-code|Document> / <notes-code|Source> /
    <notes-code|Edit source tree>.
  </warning>

  <subsection|Install>

  On Windows, open PowerShell in the repo root and run
  <notes-code|Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process Bypass>, then
  <notes-code|.\scripts\install.ps1>.

  On Linux, open a terminal in the repo root and run
  <notes-code|chmod +x scripts/install.sh>, then
  <notes-code|./scripts/install.sh>.

  After installation, restart TeXmacs and verify that the current style list
  contains <notes-code|tmarticle> and <notes-code|my-notes>.

  For the intended appearance on your setup, also enable
  <notes-code|Plain> / <notes-code|Framed theorems>.

  For the intended proof rendering on your setup, click the page layout icon
  in the upper bar and set <notes-code|Indent paragraphs> to
  <notes-code|off>.

  If you want to inspect real page boundaries, switch
  <notes-code|Document> / <notes-code|Page> / <notes-code|Type> /
  <notes-code|paper>.

  <subsection|Shortcuts>

  Native hybrid commands such as <notes-command|definition>,
  <notes-command|remark>, <notes-command|digression>, and
  <notes-command|algorithm> are the recommended insertion method.

  In math mode, type <notes-code|math u b> for the native underbrace and
  <notes-code|math o b> for the native overbrace.

  If you prefer backslash helpers, type <notes-command|ub> for underbrace,
  <notes-command|ob> for overbrace, <notes-command|m> for a matrix,
  <notes-command|sq> for a square-bracket matrix, <notes-command|tab> or
  <notes-command|tbl> for a tabular, <notes-command|f> for a figure, and
  <notes-command|todayline> for a dated separator.

  For LaTeX clipboard conversion, use <notes-code|Shift+F7> to copy the
  current selection as LaTeX and <notes-code|Shift+F8> to paste LaTeX back
  into TeXmacs.

  <section|Fast Structures>

  <subparagraph|<hrule>(02/06/2026)<hrule>>

  Tabular helper preview:

  <tabular|<tformat|<cwith|1|1|1|3|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|3|1|3|cell-lsep|0.55fn>|<cwith|1|3|1|3|cell-rsep|0.55fn>|<cwith|1|3|1|3|cell-tsep|0.25fn>|<cwith|1|3|1|3|cell-bsep|0.25fn>|<table|<row|<cell|Command>|<cell|Insert>|<cell|Context>>|<row|<cell|<notes-command|tab>>|<cell|Plain tabular>|<cell|Text>>|<row|<cell|<notes-command|tbl>>|<cell|Plain tabular>|<cell|Text>>>>>

  Figure helper preview:

  <big-figure|<tabular|<tformat|<twith|table-width|0.62par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-background|#eff6ff>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-lborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-tsep|2fn>|<cwith|1|1|1|1|cell-bsep|2fn>|<table|<row|<cell|Figure body placeholder>>>>>|Generated by the <notes-command|f> / <notes-command|fig> helper.>

  Square-bracket matrix helper preview:
  <math|<bmatrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a>|<cell|b>>|<row|<cell|c>|<cell|d>>>>>>.

  Multiline digression helper preview:

  <\digression>
    This block is intended for side comments that should not interrupt the
    main theorem/proof flow.

    It accepts multiple paragraphs and is inserted with
    <notes-command|digression>.
  </digression>

  <section|Semantic Blocks>

  This demo shows the full callout gallery currently provided by
  <notes-code|my-notes>: definition, theorem, lemma, proposition,
  corollary, example, question, warning, remark, proof, proof-of, exercise,
  solution, algorithm, idea, curiosity, summary, and digression.

  Inline semantics preview: <strong|bold>, <em|italic>,
  <underline|underline>, <notes-math-accent|x<rsup|2>+y<rsup|2>=z<rsup|2>>,
  and <notes-code|notes-code>.

  <\definition>
    A functional dependency <math|X \<rightarrow\> Y> means that each value
    of <math|X> determines a unique value of <math|Y>.
  </definition>

  <\theorem>
    If a schema is in BCNF, then every non-trivial dependency has a superkey
    on the left-hand side.
  </theorem>

  <\lemma>
    Lossless decomposition can be checked with a chase-style argument.
  </lemma>

  <\proposition>
    Every key is a superkey, but not every superkey is minimal.
  </proposition>

  <\corollary>
    A relation with a single candidate key may still violate BCNF.
  </corollary>

  <\example>
    In <math|R(A,B,C)>, the dependency <math|A \<rightarrow\> B> does not
    imply <math|A \<rightarrow\> C>.
  </example>

  <\question>
    Which dependencies survive after projecting a relation onto a subset of
    its attributes?
  </question>

  <\warning>
    Do not assume that dependency preservation and BCNF always come together
    in the same decomposition.
  </warning>

  <\remark>
    For fast study sessions, I use remarks for intuition and warnings for
    common mistakes.
  </remark>

  <\proof>
    The standard proof goes by contradiction and uses the definition of BCNF
    directly.
  </proof>

  <\proof-of|the theorem above>
    Assume a non-trivial dependency violates the BCNF condition. Then the
    left-hand side is not a superkey, which contradicts the hypothesis.
  </proof-of>

  <\exercise>
    Compute the candidate keys of <math|R(A,B,C,D)> under the dependencies
    <math|A \<rightarrow\> B> and <math|C \<rightarrow\> D>.
  </exercise>

  <\solution>
    Start from the closure of <math|A,C>. We get <math|A,C,D> after applying
    <math|C \<rightarrow\> D>, and then <math|A,B,C,D> after applying
    <math|A \<rightarrow\> B>.

    Since neither <math|A> nor <math|C> alone determines all attributes,
    <math|A,C> is a candidate key.
  </solution>

  <\algorithm>
    Input: a dependency set <math|F> and an attribute set <math|X>.

    Output: the closure <math|X<rsup|+>>.

    Repeat until no attribute changes.

    <\indent*>
      Set <math|X<rsup|+> \<leftarrow\> X>.

      For each dependency <math|Y \<rightarrow\> Z> in <math|F>, if
      <math|Y \<subseteq\> X<rsup|+>>, update
      <math|X<rsup|+> \<leftarrow\> X<rsup|+> \<cup\> Z>.
    </indent*>

    Return <math|X<rsup|+>>.
  </algorithm>

  <\idea>
    Reserve the pink blocks for future links, open problems, or lecture
    follow-ups.
  </idea>

  <\curiosity>
    The rainbow curiosity block is for compact surprising facts, historical
    asides, or mnemonic hooks.
  </curiosity>

  <\summary>
    Cyan defines concepts, blue-violet states formal results, orange gives
    examples, violet asks questions, red warns, green comments, slate handles
    proofs, and the light editor-style block is for algorithms.
  </summary>

  <\digression>
    A digression is for the moments when the lecture briefly leaves the main
    line of reasoning to add intuition, historical context, side remarks, or
    a useful mnemonic. This block is intentionally light sky-blue and
    multiline so longer side discussions stay visually separate from the main
    narrative.
  </digression>

  <section|Math Notes>

  Standard formula: <math|f<around*|(|x|)>=<frac|x<rsup|2>+1|x+1>>.

  Native wide underbrace: <math|<wide|a+b+c|\<wide-underbrace*\>>>.

  Manual accent helper: <notes-math-accent|\<Sigma\><rsub|i=1><rsup|n> i>.
</body>

<\initial>
  <\collection>
    <associate|preamble|false>
  </collection>
</initial>
